at makes the most sense. The key restructuring question in this framework is about whether parallelizing helps and whether there's a sequential dependency. For MD of transport properties, you need long trajectories (Green-Kubo correlation functions need long time). So there IS a sequential chain: the number of timesteps needed for converged viscosity. You can't easily parallelize time, but you can run multiple independent replicas (seeds) and average, which reduces statistical noise.\n\nSo the restructuring could be:\n- sequential_part: \"a Green-Kubo viscosity needs one long trajectory of ~N timesteps, computed one step at a time\" — the chain length is the number of timesteps.\n- method: \"run multiple independent replicas (different initial velocities) side by side and average the correlation functions\" — this parallelizes the noise reduction.\n\nBut then compute_axis would be \"timesteps\" or \"replicas\". Hmm.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The compute_axis is defined as \"The quantity this test varies that more parallel compute buys. If restructuring.method is not none: the chain length or the number of runs the answer needs.\"\n\nThe option's compute_open says: \"Candidates checked by GPU molecular dynamics (about 10^2 to 10^4) and simulation length per candidate.\" So the compute axis is candidates checked by MD (10^2 to 10^4). That's the number of independent runs. More parallel compute = more candidates checked simultaneously.\n\nSo the natural framing: \n- compute_axis.quantity = \"candidate fluids verified by GPU molecular dynamics\"\n- unit = \"candidates\"\n- levels = [1e2, 1e3, 1e4] (100, 1000, 10000)\n\nAnd the restructuring:\n- sequential_part: \"each candidate waits for the one before it in a single screening loop\" — how many steps: 10^4 candidates.\n- method: \"candidates are independent; verify many at once, one per GPU\" — this is \"none\"? No wait.\n\nActually re-reading: \"method ... or 'none' if the plan only runs independent work side by side or splits each step across GPUs.\"\n\nSo if my plan only runs independent candidates side by side (one per GPU), then method = \"none\". That's clean and honest: MD screening of candidates is embarrassingly parallel, there's no sequential dependency to restructure.\n\nBut the framework seems to want a \"restructuring\" story. Let me think about what the honest answer is. The screening of 10^4 candidates: each candidate MD sim is independent. The generation→filter→verify pipeline is sequential at the pipeline level but the expensive part is parallel. So method = \"none\" is legitimate: \"the plan only runs independent work side by side.\"\n\nActually, there IS a sequential part: the generative loop. Generate a batch, filter with predictor, verify top-k with MD, use results to update the generator (active learning). This is a sequential loop of \"generate → filter → verify → retrain\" rounds. Each round waits on the previous round's MD results. So sequential_part = \"the generate-filter-verify-retrain loop: each round of new candidates waits for the previous round's MD scores.\" How many steps = number of rounds (e.g., 3-5 rounds).\n\nmethod = \"why those steps need not run in order and how the plan computes them at the same time\": Actually the retrain loop is inherently sequential (you need results to retrain). But you could argue the MD verification within a round is parallel, and the generator can propose all candidates upfront (one-shot) instead of iterating. So method could be \"generate all candidates up front, filter once, then verify all in parallel\" — removing the retrain loop.\n\nThis is getting complicated. Let me make a decision and keep it simple and defensible:\n\n**Restructuring decision**: \n- The dominant cost is MD verification of ~10^3 candidates. Each candidate's MD is independent → embarrassingly parallel across GPUs.\n- sequential_part: \"Green-Kubo transport properties need a long trajectory per candidate, one timestep after another\" (chain of ~10^5-10^6 timesteps), OR \"the generate-filter-verify loop iterates round after round\".\n- method: \"none\" — because the plan runs independent candidates side by side and splits each MD step across GPUs (domain decomposition).\n\nActually, I think the cleanest is:\n- sequential_part: \"Verifying one fluid means one long molecular-dynamics trajectory, timestep after timestep; screening 10^3 candidates serially chains that 10^3 times.\"\n- method: \"none\": candidates don't depend on each other, so run all candidate simulations side by side across GPUs, one candidate per GPU.\n\nAnd compute_axis = candidates verified by MD, levels [100, 1000, 10000].\n\nThen predictions:\n- if_lever: \"Hit rate vs commercial fluids keeps rising with candidates verified (more candidates → more winners found above the best commercial fluid).\"\n- if_not: \"Hit rate plateaus or falls as predictor and force-field error dominates (extra candidates are junk or mis-scored).\"\n\nThe decision rule: measure hit rate (fraction of MD-verified candidates that beat the best commercial fluid's figure of merit) as a function of number of candidates verified. If hit rate stays roughly constant (doesn't decay) as candidates grow from 10^2 to 10^4, then more compute buys more winners → lever. If hit rate decays steeply (predictor/force-field error caps), then not lever.\n\nActually let me frame it more precisely. The question \"whether the hit rate against known commercial fluids keeps rising with more candidates\" — this is about cumulative hits, which by definition can't decrease. Hmm. \"hit rate\" might mean \"the rate at which MD verification confirms a predicted candidate beats commercial\". Let me re-read the option:\n\n\"Unknown: whether the hit rate against known commercial fluids keeps rising with more candidates, or whether predictor and force-field error caps it.\"\n\nSo \"hit rate against known commercial fluids\" = the rate at which candidates beat commercial fluids. Question: does it keep rising (i.e., does verifying more candidates yield more/better winners) or does it cap (predictor error means the predictor's top picks are mostly wrong, or force-field error means MD can't distinguish real differences).\n\nSo the decision quantity: the best candidate's figure of merit (or the count of candidates beating commercial) as a function of candidates verified. If the max figure of merit keeps improving with more candidates → lever. If it plateaus → not lever (error-capped).\n\nI'll frame:\n- decision_rule: \"Plot best-candidate cooling figure of merit vs candidates verified (100→1000→10000). Lever if the best improves by more than X% per 10x candidates (or keeps beating commercial); not lever if it plateaus within noise.\"\n\nLet me now think about the actual science to make it concrete.\n\n**Cooling figure of merit**: A common single-phase coolant FoM combines thermal conductivity k, density ρ, specific heat cp, and viscosity μ. For single-phase heat transfer, the Mouromtseff number is a figure of merit: Mo = (ρ^a k^b cp^c) / μ^d. For turbulent flow, Mouromtseff number = ρ^0.8 k^0.67 cp^0.33 / μ^0.47 (for forced convection). For laminar it's different. Let me use the Mouromtseff number as the FoM. Actually the OCP immersion requirements document likely defines something. Let me check.\n\nAlso the OCP document likely lists candidate fluids and their required properties: viscosity, thermal conductivity, flash point, dielectric constant ranges.\n\nLet me search for the OCP immersion requirements content and the eprint content.\n\n**The eprint 31955** is from ÉTS Montréal. Let me search what it is. Given the context \"SAME: a concrete version of the asker's line, kept on discovery and on transport properties (viscosity, thermal conductivity)\" — the root_link says SAME, meaning the root question is the same. The eprint is probably a thesis/paper on \"molecular dynamics for transport properties of immersion coolants\" or \"generative search for coolants\". Let me search.\n\nLet me do web searches now.\n\nKey searches:\n1. OCP ACS immersion requirements rev 2 fluid properties (viscosity, thermal conductivity, flash point, dielectric constant).\n2. eprint 31955 etsmtl (what it is).\n3. GPU molecular dynamics throughput (ns/day) for a typical system size, to estimate cost.\n4. Known commercial single-phase immersion coolant properties (e.g., Novec 7000, 3M, Shell, mineral oil, PAO, ester fluids).\n\nLet me also think about cost estimation:\n\nLambda GPU prices (from the price sheet, 1x GPU):\n- H100 SXM 80GB: $4.29/GPU/hr\n- A100 SXM 40GB: $1.99/GPU/hr\n- A6000 48GB: $1.09/GPU/hr\n\nBudget: $1,000.\n\nMD cost: For a box of ~100-500 molecules (a few thousand atoms), GROMACS/OpenMM on an A100 can do maybe 100-500 ns/day. For viscosity via Green-Kubo, you need correlation functions out to ~100 ps - 1 ns, and total simulation time maybe 10-100 ns per candidate (with multiple replicas). For thermal conductivity (heat flux autocorrelation), similar order.\n\nLet me get a proxy number. Search for \"GROMACS ns/day A100 water box 100k atoms\" or similar benchmarks.\n\nActually, a cleaner proxy: I can cite GROMACS or OpenMM benchmark papers, or NVIDIA's own benchmarks. But for the plan, I need a defensible number for \"ns/day per GPU for a ~10^3-10^4 atom system\".\n\nLet me search for concrete numbers.\n\nLet me also think about how many candidates. The plan: generate ~10^4 molecules (SMILES), filter to top ~10^3 via cheap predictors (trained on e.g. the ESP/eNanoMapper or NIST property data), then MD-verify ~10^3 candidates across ~10-16 GPUs.\n\nLet me now compute a rough budget:\n- Rate: A100 SXM 40GB at $1.99/GPU/hr, 8x GPU instance = $15.92/hr. Or use single GPUs.\n- Say each candidate needs ~12 GPU-hours of MD (e.g., 5 ns × multiple replicas at some ns/day rate). Let me get a concrete rate.\n\nLet me search for actual GROMACS benchmarks for the relevant system size.\n\nLet me do the searches in parallel now."
